"""Chapters 3 and 4 content: feasibility study, existing and proposed system, requirements, platform, user role."""
from __future__ import annotations

from doc_common import (API, BEST, DEMO, EV, LAT, MODELS, PRODUCT, REP, RET, RULES_QUALITY, RULES_SECURITY, SIZE, SMOKE_CHECKS,
                        TESTS_API, TESTS_ENGINE, UI_FLOWS, f3)


def sec_feasibility_technical(d):
    d.h2("Technical Feasibility")
    d.paras(
        "The project needs six capabilities: reading changes in several forms, static analysis of Python, measuring and scoring "
        "functions, training classifiers, answering repository questions and a web application. Each was available as open-source "
        "software; none needs a graphics processor or a paid service.",
        "**Reading changes.** Unified diffs are parsed with the Python standard library and a small hunk parser; two versions of a file "
        "are compared with `difflib`; GitHub pull requests and repositories are fetched through the public REST API with `requests`; zip "
        "files and folders are read with `zipfile` and `pathlib`. Limits (300 files per review; 1,500 files and 800 KB per file for zips and folders) bound memory use.",
        f"**Static analysis and metrics.** Python's `ast` module gives a full syntax tree, from which the analyser builds taint maps, "
        f"counts the fourteen metrics and proposes source-range rewrites. Analysing one function takes about {EV['rule_engine_seconds_per_function_ms']} ms "
        f"on average, so a large pull request is scanned in well under a second.",
        f"**Machine learning.** scikit-learn [20] and XGBoost [19] train all nine classifiers on {REP['train_size']:,} functions in "
        f"about {REP['seconds']:.0f} seconds on a CPU, including feature selection and the sweep over the number of metrics. SMOTE and Tomek "
        "links [15]-[17] are implemented in a few dozen lines of NumPy and scikit-learn code, so no extra dependency is needed.",
        "**Repository questions.** TF-IDF [22] with scikit-learn, a NetworkX graph and personalised PageRank [23] index a small "
        f"repository in {RET['index_build_ms']:.0f} ms and answer a question in about {RET['ask_median_ms']:.0f} ms.",
        f"**Web application.** FastAPI and uvicorn serve 25 routes, SQLite stores accounts and reviews, and a React 19 and TypeScript "
        f"interface built with Vite renders every state. All of this was demonstrated in the built system: {TESTS_ENGINE + TESTS_API} "
        f"automated tests, a {SMOKE_CHECKS}-check API smoke test and {UI_FLOWS} browser flows pass, and the interface builds without type errors.")
    d.p("The technical risks are small. Detection quality depends on the training data, which here are generated; the optional "
        "language-model layer depends on an external service but is not needed; and the in-memory job table does not survive a "
        "restart. The first risk matters most and is stated wherever a model result is quoted.")


def sec_feasibility_economic(d):
    d.h2("Economic Feasibility")
    d.paras(
        "Every component of the system is free and open-source software, and the system runs on an ordinary computer. The cost is "
        "therefore mainly the team's time. It was not converted into a currency amount, because no wages, purchases or hosting "
        "contracts were involved and any figure would be invented; the table states what each element needs and its fee.")
    d.table([["Item", "Choice", "Fee"],
             ["Programming and ML libraries", "Python 3.11, scikit-learn, XGBoost, pandas, NumPy, NetworkX (open source)", "None"],
             ["Web back and front end", "FastAPI, uvicorn, SQLite, bcrypt, PyJWT; React 19, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS (open source)", "None"],
             ["Hardware", "The development PC: Intel Core i5-12500 (12 threads), 15.7 GB of memory, no GPU", "Existing machine"],
             ["Training data", "Generated by `dataset.py`; no third-party data and no licence", "None"],
             ["Optional LLM layer", "Anthropic API; off by default; no key was used in the build or in the measurements", "Usage-based fee only if a user enables it; not quantified"],
             ["Hosting", "Runs locally; a server deployment was not costed", "Not measured"]],
            "Cost elements of the project", widths=[0.22, 0.52, 0.26], font=9.5)
    d.p("The effort can be gauged from the size of the product: "
        f"{SIZE['engine_py']:,} non-blank lines of Python in the engine, {SIZE['backend_py']} in the API layer, {SIZE['tests_py']} in the "
        f"tests and {SIZE['frontend_files']} TypeScript source files ({SIZE['frontend_ts']:,} lines) in the interface. The running cost is the electricity of the machine. Any saving would come from a first pass that takes a fraction of a "
        "second instead of a person's minutes, but it was not measured and is not claimed.")


def sec_feasibility_operational(d):
    d.h2("Operational Feasibility")
    d.bullets([
        "**Users.** A developer needs only a browser. Registration is by e-mail and password; the demo account `demo@autosecai.dev` lets a new user see every screen with a single button.",
        "**Setup.** `run.bat` installs the Python and npm dependencies on the first run, starts the API on port 8311 and the web application on port 5311 and opens the browser; the trained model is created automatically in about thirty seconds if it is missing.",
        f"**Everyday use.** A pull request is submitted with one button. The built-in demo change (67 added lines, 7 functions) is reviewed in a median of {LAT['median_s']:.3f} s inside the engine and {API['POST /api/reviews demo-diff -> done (polled 50 ms)']['median_ms'] / 1000:.2f} s through the API including job polling.",
        "**Maintenance.** All ports and the signing secret are in a documented `.env` file; new rules are added to the knowledge base with a CWE, a severity and a safe pattern; the model is re-trained from the Model Lab with one confirmation, and the measurement scripts re-compute the quoted figures.",
        "**Privacy.** Source code is analysed on the user's machine; it leaves only if the user enables the LLM option or fetches a GitHub pull request.",
    ])


